  "query_preview": "..\nTyphoon Choi-wan continues to intensify, and is expected to become a\nCategory 3 typhoon tonight as it continues moving away from the \nMarianas and passing near Maug to the northeast. A Typhoon Warning \nremains in effect for Alamagan, Pagan and Agrihan. Due to the \nenhancement of the monsoon surge along the southern peripheries of \nChoi-wan, TS conditions are still expected at Tinian, Saipan and \ntheir adjacent waters in the early evening hours tonight, but then \nbecoming more likely to fall below TS conditions as the night goes \non. Tropical storm force winds of 35 to 45 mph with gusts to 60 mph \nare expected in the early evening hours at Tinian and Saipan, to \nthen drop below sustained TS conditions later tonight as Typhoon \nChoi-wan (26W) continues to move away from Tinian and Saipan to the \nnorth. However, even as Typhoon Choi-wan moves away, Tinian and \nSaipan will continue to feel windy conditions into next week as the \nsurging monsoon inflow south of Choi-wan continues to affect the \narea. Meanwhile at Guam and Rota, the enhanced monsoon is already\nbringing sustained 25 to 35 mph southwesterlies, with shower and\nthunder coverage being considerably less tonight, to then taper down\nfurther as the atmosphere stabilizes over the coming days.\n\nLooking in the mid to long term, after Choi-wan's exit from the area,\nsecondary disturbances are expected to form within the leftover\nmonsoon trough, as the local environment will remain conducive for\nimproved circulation development. Even so, high uncertainty remains\nregarding wind and rain impacts at this time, but it is likely for a\nwetter period to return once again around midweek. Last but not\nleast, a drier regime may develop toward the next weekend as winds\ndiminish and a col forms overhead.",
